<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Augustine, Florida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Augustine is a city on the Atlantic coast of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, founded in 1565 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a>, who went on to destroy the French Huguenot colony at Fort Caroline. As capital of Spanish Florida, defended by the stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castillo-de-san-marcos">Castillo de San Marcos</a>, it relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and labour from the Apalachee and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> missions. It is the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida.jpg" alt="St. Augustine, Florida" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schwerdf · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Castillo_de_San_Marcos,_St._Augustine,_FL_2025-05-20.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>St. Augustine stands on the Atlantic coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, 40 miles south of downtown Jacksonville, with access to the ocean by the St. Augustine Inlet of the Matanzas River. Before the Spanish arrived in 1565 the area was inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a>, who lived in villages along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St. Johns River</a>, farmed, fished, hunted and traded with neighbouring groups. Their society had hereditary chiefs and organised ceremonies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> founded the settlement on 8 September 1565 and named it San Agustín, because his ships had first sighted land eleven days earlier on 28 August, the feast day of St. Augustine. The city served as capital of Spanish Florida for over 200 years. On 28 and 29 May 1586, soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-spanish-war-1585-1604">Anglo-Spanish War</a> began, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a> sacked it. The townspeople evacuated, and after Spanish rearguards killed their sergeant major the English razed the town.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and many Spanish settlers left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. St. Augustine became a Loyalist haven during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a>, negotiated in 1819 and ratified in 1821, ceded Florida to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and in 1824 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a> replaced St. Augustine as capital.</p><p>In 1883 Henry Flagler, a co-founder of Standard Oil, spent the winter there and decided to make the city a winter resort for wealthy northerners. In 1885 he combined short railroads into the Florida East Coast Railway. In 1963 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> seeking to integrate schools and lunch counters were met with arrests, Ku Klux Klan violence and police violence, and homes were firebombed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>St. Augustine is the county seat of St. Johns County and had a population of 14,329 in 2020. It is the oldest continuously inhabited city of European origin in the contiguous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Castillo de San Marcos, the city's 17th-century Spanish fort, is a major attraction, and St. George Street, a pedestrian street, has over 30 historic houses and attractions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Augustine, Florida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Franciscan missions began in 1633, and many Apalachee became Catholic, sending maize and labour to <b>St Augustine</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1565 Philip II sent Pedro Menéndez de Avilés, who founded <b>St Augustine</b>, captured Fort Caroline and executed Ribault and his shipwrecked men at Matanzas Inlet.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li><li><blockquote>When French Huguenots built Fort Caroline in French Florida, Philip II sent Pedro Menéndez de Avilés, who founded <b>St Augustine</b> in 1565 and destroyed the French colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a></li></ul></section>
